    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n[It's fine if you don't think this belongs in the source repo, I primarily implemented it for myself and it can stay in my fork if you don't like it]\r\n\r\nI've implemented the \"Fly Mode\" functionality from [jiangmiao/auto-pairs](https://github.com/jiangmiao/auto-pairs). It allows you to easily jump out of a pair of brackets no matter where you are. I personally find it very convenient, especially when dealing with nested brackets of different kinds.",
    "closed_at": "2021-11-11T19:46:12Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thank you for this contribution!\r\n\r\nI personally never really found fly mode convenient enough, so it wasn't included. Is it really that useful?\r\n\r\nFor me there are several concerns here:\r\n- It somewhat contradicts the overall idea that functionality depends only on cursor's neighborhood.\r\n- It also makes function names `close()` and `closeopen()` not quite aligned.\r\n- In 'jiangmiao/auto-pairs' it works only for `)`, `}`, `]`. So I wonder if `config.fly_mode` should be supplied as table with closing symbols for which fly mode will be active.\r\n- In original functionality there is also a `AutoPairsShortcutBackInsert` for when flying was not intended. Is current functionality sufficiently useful for you without this behavior?\r\n\r\nSo overall I don't see a problem with (and not opposed to) functionality itself. How to nicely fit it inside current framework, that is an issue. I'll ponder on it for a bit (*quite* a bit actually, as there are other plans for this plugin) and see what I can come up with. If you have any suggestions on these concerns, I'll gladly hear them!",
            "created_at": "2021-11-02T08:23:47Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/pull/7#issuecomment-957197913",
            "id": 957197913,
