        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I find that it makes opening and closing brackets more symmetrical. When inserting an opening brace, it's always inserted as a pair, and if you don't want this, you use `<c-v>`. Analogically, a closing brace will always close a pair (that is, go past an already inserted closing brace) and if you don't want that, you can use `<c-v>`.\r\n\r\n> It somewhat contradicts the overall idea that functionality depends only on cursor's neighborhood.\r\n\r\nI did find it weird in this way when I learned about it in auto-pairs, but I decided to try it and started to see the usefulness.\r\n\r\n> It also makes function names close() and closeopen() not quite aligned.\r\n\r\nThat's true, but I don't really expect self-paired delimiters to behave consistently with paired delimiters anyway. Maybe your expectations are different. I'd kind of unfortunate that both are commonly used in programming.\r\n\r\n> In 'jiangmiao/auto-pairs' it works only for ), }, ]. So I wonder if config.fly_mode should be supplied as table with closing symbols for which fly mode will be active.\r\n\r\nThat's a good idea. Maybe someone will want to be able to quickly jump to the end of a block in C-like languages, but not find the functionality useful for parentheses.\r\n\r\n> In original functionality there is also a AutoPairsShortcutBackInsert for when flying was not intended. Is current functionality sufficiently useful for you without this behavior?\r\n\r\nTo be honest, I've never used it and forgot that it exists. I just use `<c-v>` when I want to insert an unpaired closing bracket.",
